## 13. Measure trust, suitability, and customer outcomes

### Commercial metrics

- verified and funded accounts;
- cost per funded account;
- net contributions rather than gross deposits;
- 30-, 90-, and 180-day retention;
- assets and revenue by customer cohort;
- contribution consistency;
- goal completion;
- withdrawal and reactivation patterns;
- partner-channel economics;
- progression from education to investment.

### Product and trust metrics

- percentage of users understanding that capital is at risk;
- acceptance and rejection of AI recommendations;
- use of “why this recommendation” explanations;
- human-support escalation;
- recommendation override rate;
- automation opt-out rate;
- family-feature consent revocation;
- trust and comprehension by language and cohort;
- complaint resolution time;
- app-store and social reports of fraud or impersonation.

### Compliance and Sharia metrics

- unsuitable recommendation rate;
- transactions blocked by compliance constraints;
- Sharia-screening exceptions;
- purification accuracy and timeliness;
- disclosure defects;
- KYC and sanctions performance;
- suspicious-activity escalation;
- privacy and cybersecurity incidents;
- model-validation findings;
- regulator or Sharia-board remediation;
- time to correct affected customer accounts.

### Outcome metric

The core business metric should be:

> **Incremental customer savings and appropriately risk-adjusted investment persistence attributable to the app, relative to full compliance, partnership, servicing, remediation, and acquisition cost.**

A large number of automated transfers is not success if customers misunderstand risk, withdraw under distress, or feel pressured by family or algorithmic nudges.

